id: core/choice-and-move-selection
title: "choice and move selection"
kind: governance-policy
status: working-draft-0.1
updated: 2026-09-01

purpose: >
  Close the executable seam between an attributed Orientation Map and an
  authorized transition without allowing a ranking score, fluent model output,
  interface click, or accepted representation to silently become rightful
  choice or effect authority.

position_in_the_loop: >
  Relationship and Arena condition orientation. Orientation exposes possible
  inquiries, holds, intentions, and moves. Move selection may compare and
  recommend. A rightful principal may then choose. A separate authority record
  must permit an exact effect before a delegate executes it. Consequence, proof,
  and update remain later and distinct.

canonical_path:
  - orientation-map-revision
  - attributed-move-candidates
  - move-selection-receipt-or-explicit-hold
  - rightful-choice-event
  - separate-authority-resolution
  - transition-intent
  - execution-or-delegation
  - consequence
  - proof-adjudication
  - authorized-state-and-relationship-update

non_collapses:
  - "salience is not relevance, meaning, truth, importance, or permission"
  - "orientation is not recommendation"
  - "recommendation is not choice"
  - "choice is not authorization"
  - "authorization is not execution"
  - "capability is not permission"
  - "execution is not consequence"
  - "consequence is not proof"
  - "proof is not update"
  - "acceptance is not truth"
  - "a View is not ontology or canonical state"

move_candidate:
  definition: >
    A move candidate is an attributed proposal for one bounded transition in the
    active Arena. It names the expected consequence, required authority,
    reversibility, proofability, cost, risk, option preservation, and sources.
  boundary: >
    A move candidate may be generated by a person, model, agent, policy, or
    historical record. Its origin and confidence never grant decision rights.

selection_policy:
  output_schema: schemas/move-selection-receipt.v0.1.schema.json
  policy_version: yawn.move-selection.v0.1
  passes:
    - id: hard-gates
      order: 1
      checks:
        - immediate-safety-or-stability
        - authority-or-consent
        - privacy-visibility-or-egress
        - source-or-provenance
        - proof-or-falsifier-integrity
      rule: >
        An active or unknown material gate excludes the affected move or causes
        an explicit hold. No score, urgency, confidence, preference, or expected
        upside may bypass it.
    - id: situated-fit
      order: 2
      checks:
        - fit-to-current-orientation
        - fit-to-attributed-goals-and-values
        - affected-relationship-fit
        - smallest-sufficient-commitment
      rule: >
        Fit is evaluated against the current time-indexed Orientation Map, not a
        generic optimization objective or an inferred permanent identity.
    - id: learning-and-recovery
      order: 3
      checks:
        - information-gain
        - proofability
        - option-preservation
        - reversibility
        - risk
        - cost
      rule: >
        These signals compare candidates; they do not define human meaning or
        authorize the result.
  tie_break_order:
    - clearer-hard-gates
    - higher-proofability
    - greater-option-preservation
    - greater-reversibility
    - lower-risk
    - lower-cost
    - lexicographic-candidate-id
  allowed_results:
    - one-non-authoritative-move-proposal
    - explicit-hold-with-reopen-condition

move_selection_receipt:
  schema_ref: schemas/move-selection-receipt.v0.1.schema.json
  canonical_state: false
  epistemic_status: inferred
  required_flags:
    choiceRequired: true
    authorizationRequired: true
  proves: >
    Given the attributed effective inputs and policy version, the receipt makes
    the comparison and proposed result replayable.
  does_not_prove:
    - authenticity of every historical input
    - that the selected move is morally or empirically correct
    - that the principal chose it
    - that authority exists
    - that execution occurred
    - that the expected consequence followed

choice_event:
  schema_ref: schemas/choice-event.v0.1.schema.json
  definition: >
    An attributed event in which the rightful principal selects one candidate,
    rejects all candidates, or holds. The principal may choose differently from
    the recommendation and may give no rationale beyond a bounded private
    record where appropriate.
  authority_rule: >
    A choice event records authorship of direction. It never grants an external
    effect. Its authorization.grantedByThisEvent and effectAuthorized fields
    remain false; any authority reference is separately resolved.

relation_to_existing_contracts:
  orientation: core/orientation.yawn
  orientation_map: schemas/orientation-map.v0.1.schema.json
  inquiry_selection: core/inquiry-selection.yawn
  state_transition: core/state-transition.yawn
  transition_intent: schemas/transition-intent.v1.schema.json
  execution_relationship: schemas/execution-relationship.v1.schema.json
  action_policy: schemas/action-policy.v1.schema.json
  action_receipt: schemas/action-receipt.v1.schema.json
  proof_and_boundary: core/proof-and-boundary.yawn
  rule: >
    This draft does not replace those contracts. It gives typed identities to
    the recommendation and choice references already implied by transition
    intent and the decision template.

interface_rule: >
  A button labeled Accept may accept a proposed representation into a local or
  canonical orientation review state. It must not be rendered or persisted as a
  rightful move choice unless the interface explicitly binds the exact move
  candidate, current selection receipt, principal identity, and choice event.
  Even then, effect authority remains separately required.

swarm_rule: >
  Parent, child, sibling, or provider agents may propose and compare moves. A
  child inherits no terminal goal, truth, consent, privacy widening, spending
  authority, mutation authority, or delegation power merely from placement in
  the holarchy. Every executable effect traces to a rightful grant and returns a
  consequence receipt to the relationship that authorized it.

proof:
  schemas:
    - schemas/move-selection-receipt.v0.1.schema.json
    - schemas/choice-event.v0.1.schema.json
  fixture: fixtures/choice-and-move-selection.v0.1.json
  executable: lib/choice-and-move-selection-v0.1.mjs
  validator: scripts/validate-choice-and-move-selection-v0.1.mjs
  tests: tests/choice-and-move-selection-v0.1.test.mjs
  status: contract-defined-working-draft

open_lacunae:
  - calibrate comparison weights against principal-reviewed real episodes
  - define authenticated append-only application and current-head conflict rules
  - bind choice-event references into a future additive transition-intent version
  - test multi-principal conflict, refusal, and negotiated-choice cases
  - compare this selector against no ranking, random order, and human-only review

boundary:
  - "This contract is not a universal utility function."
  - "A high score is not meaning, correctness, or permission."
  - "Waiting, inquiry, refusal, exit, and deliberate non-action remain valid."
  - "The rightful principal can reject the proposed move or the framing itself."
  - "No record in this draft activates a bot, grants authority, executes an effect, or updates canonical state."
